// Practice quiz: File permissions
// Formative, low-stakes retention practice. Titles name the objective id each item practises.

$CATEGORY: git-linux-ai-foundations/linux-foundations/file-permissions

::o1 group letters::ls -l shows report.txt as -rw-rw-r--. What can members of the file's group do? {
=Read and write it, but not execute it #Correct. After the type character, the second set of three, rw-, is the group: read and write, no execute.
~Read it only #Not quite. r-- is the last set, which applies to everyone else.
~Read, write and execute it #Not quite. That would be rwx; the group set here ends in -.
~Nothing #Not quite. --- would mean nothing; the group set here is rw-.
}

::o1 file or directory::ls -l shows drwxr-xr-x for an entry named build. What does the first character tell you? {
=build is a directory #Correct. The first character is the type: d for a directory, - for a regular file.
~build is read-only #Not quite. The first character is the type, not a permission.
~build can be deleted by anyone #Not quite. The first character is the type; the three sets after it hold the permissions.
~build is a hidden file #Not quite. Hidden names start with a dot; the first character of the string is the type.
}

::o2 numeric mode::Which command gives the owner read and write, the group read only, and everyone else nothing (rw-r-----)? {
=chmod 640 notes.txt #Correct. r \= 4, w \= 2, x \= 1, so rw- is 6, r-- is 4 and --- is 0.
~chmod 644 notes.txt #Not quite. The final 4 would let everyone else read it.
~chmod 460 notes.txt #Not quite. Digits go owner, group, others, so this gives the owner read only.
~chmod 750 notes.txt #Not quite. 7 and 5 add execute for the owner and the group.
}

::o3 run directly or through bash::build.sh is -rw-r--r-- and you own it. Which command runs it without changing any permission? {
=bash build.sh #Correct. bash only needs to read the file to run its commands, and you have r.
~./build.sh #Not quite. Running a file directly as ./build.sh needs x, which no one has, so the shell answers Permission denied.
~build.sh #Not quite. Without ./ the shell searches the PATH folders for a command named build.sh, and it still needs x.
~sudo cat build.sh #Not quite. cat prints the file; it does not run it.
}

::o3 what x adds::After chmod 750 build.sh, which way of running it newly works for its owner? {
=./build.sh, because the owner now has x #Correct. Running a file directly needs execute permission; 7 gives the owner rwx.
~bash build.sh, which needed x before #Not quite. bash build.sh only needs read permission, so it already worked.
~Running it as any other user #Not quite. The final 0 gives everyone else no permissions at all.
~Opening it in an editor #Not quite. Editing needs read and write; it does not involve x.
}
